id "3549dac9-6168-561f-967d-c8c6f0ff3ecf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100640 #edb/ref 1069 "e-339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100640 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/e3/e3feb58766bbbb4dc2ed0cd738c7a5d74d31a6d3bbc2e210010b9e5ecbc21da8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100640 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b188b17-3d3f-5063-b12b-cdc6b58191d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100640 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points:\n\n$$\nx=-1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1$. Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$, the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27cfed2793c78d0abaa91c4d5480724303ebf729fa22befbd6142e8e54039e64.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=-1$. Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and goes down to the right of $x=1$. Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) > 0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) < 0$. So, $x =1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\n![](images/15/15842026cf3d019dcfd31c0f4f9aad54a4c8465dc1464feaa2d07b45e109ef74.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/0f/0f7e45db7621e8ecd7d915d49d14a8656070365023f12d7239c435f7da8f72fc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100641 #edb/ref 1069 "e-340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100641 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/23/23c5620bdd59a6359eaa9bad6580b4b844237bead64b7138f7472b65c7e17c1b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100641 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dec0e17e-d9c6-5b6e-994f-717fb60b75fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100641 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points:\n\n$$\nx=1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=2\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2, \\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=2$. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=1$.Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$, the point at $x=2$ must be lower than the point at $x=1$.\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/e3/e3a15ad7af8061bb800c5536fe054594402c7898ef7485a8a4165aff9cdc272d.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=1$.Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(2,\\infty)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=2$.Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 3$, we must have $f''(x) > 0$. So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,3)$. Also, since $f'(x)$ is constant and positive to the right of $x = 3$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing straight line on $(3,\\infty)$.\n\n![](images/20/200f45be73f0c38d5cab4099ac83ab5d80022539e17cd0996f1ab0228a90a89e.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/16/16aff3b702623f3b6339cacfcdac4c9b43900e58f1228481d168b8b0535e1412.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100766 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100766 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below. Given that $f(0)=0$, sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/ef/ef91945c0b2261785677c4c8ba7e6d202f62bc6c2da20a213bcba03cbcad8556.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100766 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42f35d1e-5f81-5616-8d07-6c7bcf42d107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100766 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:\n$x=-2$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line $x=-2$.Since we are told that $f(0)=0$, we can draw that point on the graph.Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-2,\\infty)$, the point at $x=-2$ must be lower than the point at $x=0$.\n\n![](images/4b/4b599481e7a73ec38b6f0f627b1b9a551c65e6cd3875b3789f0e7ebfa03eb786.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical point:Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-\\infty,-2)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-2,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-2$.\n\n![](images/1a/1ae0017fd07d6e9fa04f233ca31ab89a07260a5dbc5557e4c8ae35ff91d61f43.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/c2/c215907aa0cc156e92ab3b6710d844e774df3149656fc2f71f27ee01db87382e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100767 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100767 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f'(x)= \\mid x+1 \\mid$, sketch a possible graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100767 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1fe80bf-85bd-590f-806b-1d22d818a696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100767 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/57/57937f50c8adac57cd0a8b131ae2605466fe5e87e85ba324977a95b2f2c7a006.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x=-1$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{no extr.}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1$, as shown below.![](images/29/296d0c0e63bedc37841aa914e0fb07320fff634ea1f6ac019a84cc47b87966b4.png)\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and on $(-1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down the left of $x=-1$ and goes up to the right of $x=-1$. Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) < 0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) > 0$. So, $x =-1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.So, we can draw as follows:![](images/1c/1c4fb55a07e1b55448ec2a2ada4fea07f363542cdf04a8676c90938b5c4f7fbe.png)\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:![](images/df/dfeb793809943dc496cc1f47b26f9b20aa3403b6d999787a7444ede605547226.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100829 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100829 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100829 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100829 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4cad1e6a-c137-573e-8cad-c992931540a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100830 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100830 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100830 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching a Continuous Function Given the Graph of its Non-Smooth Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100830 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100830 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bfa3432b-2bf3-51c8-befb-0299783e7111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100935 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100935 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100935 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative and a Point on the Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100935 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100935 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "15bfb4b0-21fc-577d-9d5e-bcd7e06b2be1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100936 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100936 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100936 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching a Continuous Function Given its Derivative in Algebraic Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100936 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100936 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "07cdfef0-85d9-5197-b2f2-2f9052d83a3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1045 "Sketching a Function From the Graph of its Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101062 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "937f8fae-dff7-5847-9177-41361303fc87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101308 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101308 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101308 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5e/5e1d4dbd07dbf4e7f06b5ef2dd01ae2ef5fa82f3a5a78fb470388fad8b18af1e.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y = f(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101308 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "805cd960-0e6c-5413-ba15-d13411a05c8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101309 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101309 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101309 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c1/c1b92084149ea54a3d309abf1a441c0f6855b15dc999f18c0108b02130d97ce8.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y = f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y = f(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101309 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72e69ce6-788b-5045-a97d-e5fb2ac0d0b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101445 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f^{′}(x) =-\\mid x + 2 \\mid$, which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y = f(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0894de1-f2ec-5434-b75f-84e94baff54e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186100035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101479 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101479 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/58/58ebecdd8284dff6e807465f29c890bd87ec763896242cbff2bd1d18a4728f49.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y = f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y = f(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101479 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bea5ecd5-1bd7-56d0-b590-dc8806f75f22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101610 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101610 #edb/ref 1061 1244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7be92f4e-9fd4-54b7-90b1-2736d279c774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101611 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101611 #edb/ref 1061 1245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101611 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "abfe8a46-b88e-5eee-890a-9ec5626d0c97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101611 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101612 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101612 #edb/ref 1061 1249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101612 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d52aea78-22ea-5b8c-9d69-b2315845177a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101612 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101613 #edb/ref 1061 1250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d90ff3ad-345d-5dad-a7d4-7fe37502b461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101778 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101778 #edb/ref 1061 25582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101778 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66862a27-44fc-5a71-aad7-3b732213cf51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101778 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101779 #edb/ref 1061 25585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101779 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c09e702e-aaa5-5a13-a34d-af738e660698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101779 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102005 #edb/ref 1064 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Let's use this to sketch a possible graph of the original function, $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/6c/6cd6c0fa999d571b54b7408856ecc8d091c2f4bb16b9f7bf85eb7f91b05629e4.png)\n\nRecall that the critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points:\n\n$$\nx=-3\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-3$ and $x=1$. Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$, the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-3$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb0de4712344b43d32343f4d9fbc11d3dbc5c96df858f27bee599a3ffdd448ce.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-3)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-3$.Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1$.\n\n![](images/34/3498651b6a642f59068f23e8ed9ddd211bfb76ab4d0bf8e28cefd84d1a4b2aed.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/25/257203aa25e423bf82ce745a6a8a3563aa80e414e9dfadf0be64629624cbce0e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102005 #edb/ref 1065 651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102005 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0173ff8c-84c4-5761-9aad-f4e4362d1e7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102005 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102006 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's draw our possible function $y=f(x)$ once more. The graph of its derivative $y=f'(x)$ is also shown.\n\n![](images/18/189c181d3767960f39fc0cfcb06dac5bf6fca07e303b11999307551d081ffc1e.png)\n\nIn reality, there are infinitely many possible graphs of $f(x)$ that can be drawn if we are given only the graph of $f'(x)$. We can always shift the graph up and down as we please, because the slopes of the tangent lines do not change. For the problem above, any of the following graphs would be valid:\n\n![](images/e4/e49f65d7306192d73001552084f06cf39731f38f3ccc14dd6b2f5f7641ec43aa.png)\n\nIn this topic, we will focus on sketching a *possible* graph of $y=f(x)$ given the graph of $y=f'(x)$. But bear in mind that whatever $y=f(x)$ we sketch, we can get an equally valid answer by shifting or $y=f(x)$ curve up or down by an arbitrary amount.\n\nThe only exception is if we're given a point on the curve $y=f(x)$. If we're given a point, this has the effect of *fixing* our answer to a particular curve. We'll see an example of this next." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102006 #edb/ref 1065 653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102006 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aed3a1d0-c855-5799-a9a4-76d3c17c10bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102006 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Uniqueness in Sketching a Function Given the Graph of Its Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
